**Proof.** Suppose for contradiction that $n \mid a_i(a_{i+1}-1)$ for all $i=1,\dots,k$ (with $a_{k+1}=a_1$).

**Step 1: A divisibility relation.** For each $i$, let $g_i = \gcd(a_i, n)$. Write $a_i = g_i \cdot a_i'$ and $n = g_i \cdot n_i'$ where $\gcd(a_i', n_i')=1$. From $n \mid a_i(a_{i+1}-1)$ we get $n_i' \mid a_i'(a_{i+1}-1)$. Since $\gcd(a_i', n_i')=1$, this gives $n_i' \mid (a_{i+1}-1)$, i.e.,

$$\frac{n}{g_i} \mid (a_{i+1}-1). \tag{1}$$

So $a_{i+1} \equiv 1 \pmod{n/g_i}$, which implies $\gcd(a_{i+1},\, n/g_i)=1$.

**Step 2: The $g_i$ are all equal.** Since $n = g_i \cdot (n/g_i)$ and $\gcd(a_{i+1},\, n/g_i)=1$, we use the fact that $\gcd(x, ab)=\gcd(x,a)$ whenever $\gcd(x,b)=1$ to obtain:

$$g_{i+1} = \gcd(a_{i+1},\, n) = \gcd(a_{i+1},\, g_i).$$

Thus $g_{i+1} \mid g_i$ for every $i$. Chaining around the cycle:

$$g_1 \mid g_k \mid g_{k-1} \mid \cdots \mid g_2 \mid g_1,$$

so all $g_i$ are equal; call the common value $g$.

**Step 3: Deriving the contradiction.** From (1), $n/g \mid (a_j - 1)$ for all $j$, so

$$a_j \equiv 1 \pmod{n/g} \quad \text{for all } j. \tag{2}$$

This gives $\gcd(a_j, n/g)=1$, hence $\gcd(a_j, n) = \gcd(a_j, g)$. Since $\gcd(a_j,n)=g$, we get $g \mid a_j$, i.e.,

$$a_j \equiv 0 \pmod{g} \quad \text{for all } j. \tag{3}$$

Each $a_j$ satisfies the simultaneous congruences (2) and (3):

$$a_j \equiv 0 \pmod{g}, \qquad a_j \equiv 1 \pmod{n/g}.$$

By the Chinese Remainder Theorem, this system has a solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\gcd(g,\, n/g) \mid (1-0)=1$, i.e., $\gcd(g, n/g)=1$. We distinguish two cases:

- **If $\gcd(g, n/g) = 1$:** the solution is unique modulo $n$, so there is exactly one value in $\{1,\dots,n\}$ satisfying both congruences. All $a_j$ equal this value, contradicting the hypothesis that the $a_j$ are distinct with $k > 1$.

- **If $\gcd(g, n/g) > 1$:** the system has no solution at all, contradicting the existence of the integers $a_j$.

In both cases we reach a contradiction. Therefore our assumption was false: there exists some $i \le k$ such that $n \nmid a_i(a_{i+1}-1)$. $\blacksquare$

